  "summary": "The Tempe City Council voted 6-1 to ban police purchase, use, and access of automated license plate reader systems and data, with limited exceptions, making Tempe the first Arizona city to enact such a ban. Tempe had already canceled its Flock Safety contract in August; the new ordinance requires destruction of existing ALPR data and sets employee discipline for violations, over the objection of the police chief.",
  "key_quotes": [
    {
      "quote": "In a 6-1 vote, the council passed a law to prohibit the city and law enforcement from purchasing, using, and accessing ALPR systems and data except in limited circumstances.",
      "paragraph_idx": 4
    },
    {
      "quote": "In August, Tempe canceled its contract with ALPR vendor Flock Safety and disabled the cameras citywide.",
      "paragraph_idx": 5
    },
    {
      "quote": "The Tempe Police Department does not support that ban, and cannot support this ordinance as written",
      "paragraph_idx": 14
    },
    {
      "quote": "This, as far as I'm aware, is the first ban in the nation that affects both stationary and mobile ALPRs.",
      "paragraph_idx": 18
    }
  ],
